2822265625, -0.01505279541015625, -0.0219879150390625, 0.00662994384765625, -0.0179443359375, -0.005023956298828125, -0.003559112548828125, 0.0203399658203125, -0.005908966064453125, 0.020233154296875, -0.0096435546875, 0.00951385498046875, 0.0246124267578125, 0.0207061767578125, 0.002933502197265625, -0.0219573974609375, 0.0149993896484375, -0.007411956787109375, 0.014801025390625, -0.015777587890625, 0.004795074462890625, -0.0135040283203125, -0.03240966796875, -0.0015687942504882812, -0.0180511474609375, -0.006458282470703125, -0.002208709716796875, 0.0399169921875, 0.01399993896484375, 0.0020084381103515625, -0.0196685791015625, 0.0083465576171875, 0.01177978515625, 0.00241851806640625, -0.0011920928955078125, 0.0017747879028320312, -0.0179595947265625, 0.010406494140625, -0.0264739990234375, -0.00936126708984375, -0.00667572021484375, -0.0276336669921875, 0.0234222412109375, -0.0484619140625, 0.0110931396484375, -0.0211334228515625, -0.023529052734375, 0.0252227783203125, -0.0247344970703125, -0.0167236328125, -0.0072784423828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that incrementally learn from a stream of tasks and update a textual memory bank to improve over time\u2014have demonstrated strong task performance. However, their *reliability aspects*, specifically the consistency and robustness of their performance across different sources of stochasticity and variable task sequences, remain underexplored. This work seeks to fill this gap by systematically posing and addressing the following research questions: (1) How consistent is the performance of memory-based self-improving agents under different runs and task orderings? (2) What are the main sources of fragility or instability in these agents' improvement processes? (3) Can explicit specification and feedback mitigate these weaknesses?\n\nTo this end, we re-evaluate two representative memory-based methods: (a) episodic memory augmentation for language agents (e.g., agents that append text summaries or mistakes to a persistent bank between episodes as in [Wang et al., 2023]), and (b) reinforcement learning (RL) agents that retrieve and query a textual archive of past interactions to inform policy updates (following approaches like [Lee et al., 2022]). Both classes of agents update a persistent memory store after each completed task, which subsequently informs action selection or planning on new tasks.\n\nFor evaluation, we target both *procedural tasks* (e.g., sequencing actions to prepare virtual objects, such as assembling tools in a simulated environment) and *reasoning-heavy challenges* (e.g., multi-step arithmetic word problems or symbolic manipulation puzzles). Performance is measured by (i) task completion rate (the proportion of tasks for which the final goal state is achieved according to environment criteria) and (ii) average episode reward (accumulated numeric reward provided by the environment, specific to each task domain). We conduct ten independent training/evaluation runs per configuration and systematically randomize task sequences using uniform sampling without replacement, rather than following a fixed or ascending-difficulty schedule.\n\nOur results reveal two primary weaknesses in current approaches. First, agent performance exhibits substantial variance: under identical conditions, completion rates across seeds range from 55% to 88% (standard deviations up to 15%), with variance especially pronounced on multi-step logical reasoning tasks. Introducing a self-improving loop further amplifies run-to-run performance unpredictability, as memory errors compound. Second, improvement trajectories are highly sensitive to task order: with randomly shuffled task orders, mean gains drop by 10\u201318% across domains, and in certain runs improvement is entirely absent. Notably, prior work often adopts fixed or ascending-difficulty task orders that unintentionally create an *implicit curriculum*\u2014a practice that, as observed in curriculum learning literature (e.g., [Bengio et al., 2009]), facilitates incremental mastery. When this scaffold is removed by task shuffling, many agents fail to generalize or recover from early errors.\n\nTo further diagnose the fragility sources, we manually inspect agent memory traces and find a prevalence of misinterpretations arising from ambiguous or underspecified task instructions and feedback. We hypothesize that the absence of explicit task structure leaves agents prone to memory drift and confusion. To test this, we intervene in memory construction by (i) appending structured rubrics\u2014stepwise textual checklists explicitly delineating task success and failure criteria\u2014to agent memories, and (ii) providing programmatic, fine-grained environment feedback (e.g., marking specific subtasks as passed or failed, rather than a single binary outcome). Compared to the original setup (where only minimal, end-state feedback was given), these enhancements consistently increase worst-case completion rates by 5\u20138%, especially mitigating total failures. However, significant unpredictability and heightened sensitivity to task order persist, indicating that lack of specification is only one factor among several.\n\nKey remaining gaps include high between-run variance (completion rates with ranges exceeding 30% in some domains) and unrecoverable agent failures under challenging randomized task orderings. Evaluation on fixed orders or reporting only mean scores can thus obscure substantial instability.\n\n**Based on these findings, we recommend:**\n- (1) Future evaluations of self-improving agents should report both mean and variance of metrics across multiple random seeds and randomized task orderings.\n- (2) Benchmark protocols should minimize implicit curricula by randomizing task sequences and including stress-test splits with deliberately difficult orderings.\n- (3) Agent and environment interfaces should explicitly support specification (e.g., via structured rubrics and rich programmatic feedback) for both agent comprehension and external diagnosis or intervention, especially to catch memory-based failures.\n\nWe hope that these actionable recommendations will guide the development of more robust, transparent, and trustworthy memory-based self-improving agents."}}